WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.302
-Our next guest is the author of

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:03.969
the critically acclaimed
book "Ohio."

00:00:03.969 --> 00:00:06.172
His latest novel, "The Deluge,"
was published today

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:07.874
and is available wherever
you buy your books.

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:10.743
Please welcome to the show
Stephen Markley, everybody.

00:00:10.743 --> 00:00:12.912
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:23.389 --> 00:00:26.059
Welcome to the show.
I am thrilled to have you here.

00:00:26.059 --> 00:00:30.396
I loved your book "Ohio," which
is also a long, wonderful book.

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:32.464
I was surprised to learn
that you had sort of

00:00:32.464 --> 00:00:35.501
started this book
even before you started "Ohio."

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:37.202
This is a book 12 years
in the making.

00:00:37.202 --> 00:00:40.040
-Yeah, started both of them
at about the same time,

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.042
set aside "The Deluge"
to finish "Ohio,"

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:45.111
came back to it
with about 40% of it done.

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:46.412
So 13 years
in the making, really.

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:47.580
-13 years in the making,

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.150
and it is about a
deteriorating climate situation.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.085
And I'm wondering,
when you write a book about

00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:57.156
our climate in 2012, do you have
to go back and change

00:00:57.156 --> 00:01:00.126
things based on
the fact that, obviously,

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:02.895
it's happening -- Year in and
year out, it's getting worse.

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:04.963
-The hardest part about
the editing process

00:01:04.963 --> 00:01:07.800
was making the zippers of
the teeth fit together, right?

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.635
So it had to meet the moment.

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:12.071
So I'm constantly revising in
these last three years

00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:15.074
based on stuff that's happening
in the news, you know?

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:18.244
The situation where plot points
I'm concocting in 2012

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:20.280
are suddenly headlines in 2022.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.781
And even with, like,
just a small example,

00:01:22.781 --> 00:01:25.117
Russia's invasion of Ukraine,
I'm, like, racing back

00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:27.987
through the book on fourth pass
to try to account for that,

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:30.389
because I had it
happening in 2032.

00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:33.326
-So you were a little late.
Also very impressive.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:35.161
Congratulations,
but just a little late.

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.997
Because this takes place
from 2013 to 2040.

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:39.465
-Yeah.

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:43.969
-And, again, it's this sprawling
book with all these characters.

00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:45.504
And, you know, "Ohio,"

00:01:45.504 --> 00:01:47.173
a lot of dark things
happen in that book, as well,

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:48.741
but one of the reasons
I couldn't put it down is,

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:50.943
I cared so much about
the characters.

00:01:50.943 --> 00:01:53.479
And is that one of the keys
when you write a book

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:54.780
about this, as well?

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.483
Obviously, it's very scary stuff
you're writing about,

00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:59.652
but what makes you
not want to put it down is,

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:01.120
you care about the
people in the book.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.222
-It's scary
because it's abstract.

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:06.058
So I feel like the way in
is always through human beings.

00:02:06.058 --> 00:02:10.196
And the point of a book like
this is to emotionally reorient

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:12.565
the reader around
what's happening

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:14.166
so we can actually
feel in our hearts

00:02:14.166 --> 00:02:16.468
what the stakes of
this moment actually are.

00:02:16.468 --> 00:02:18.437
-You have climate scientists
in the book.

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:20.673
Obviously, there's a great
amount of science in the book.

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:23.642
You, I'm assuming,
talked to many of them.

00:02:23.642 --> 00:02:26.179
-Just a few, yeah. Yeah.

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:29.082
Yeah, I mean one of the big
pleasures of the process was,

00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:33.319
I met James Hansen, who is the
NASA scientist who, in 1988,

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:35.154
testified before Congress,
and he was one of

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:37.723
the first people to sort of
bring climate change

00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:39.558
to the public's attention.

00:02:39.558 --> 00:02:41.494
So, he was gracious enough
to give me an hour,

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:42.929
and you don't even know
what you're looking for

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:45.097
in those moments,
but definitely shards

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:47.433
of that person ended up
in a character in the book.

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.535
-Do they worry --
Do scientists worry

00:02:49.535 --> 00:02:51.237
when they talk to
a fiction writer

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:53.405
that you're going to take
what they say

00:02:53.405 --> 00:02:56.242
and then put it on
the page wrong?

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:59.245
-I'm sure, yes,
but so does the news media.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.546
SO, like --
-Yeah, exactly.

00:03:00.546 --> 00:03:02.248
I guess that's probably
worse, right?

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:04.984
Even in, you know --
Even in this book,

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:09.154
a lot of the scientists
are frustrated by the fact that

00:03:09.154 --> 00:03:10.456
they feel as though
they're trying to be

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:12.225
as clear as possible,
and the news media

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:15.361
often boils it down to the place
where it's no longer

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.362
scientifically accurate.

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:17.530
-Right, right.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.331
Well, I think, like,
in approaching --

00:03:19.331 --> 00:03:21.267
All the science is happening
in the future, right?

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.035
So I'm making guesses,
educated guesses,

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:24.270
about where we're going.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.973
So there's that added layer
of fictionalizing

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:29.174
our very, very
serious situation.

00:03:29.174 --> 00:03:32.311
-You got some very high praise,
which I think speaks to

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:34.681
the readability of this book,
because I would argue

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:39.151
the most readable author in the
last 100 years is Stephen King.

00:03:39.151 --> 00:03:41.954
Stephen King wrote about
this book,

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:44.290
that it is, simply put,
a modern classic.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.458
If you read this,
you'll never forget it.

00:03:46.458 --> 00:03:48.127
Coming in January.
I'll remind you.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:50.763
Prophetic, terrifying,
uplifting.

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:52.497
What was it like
when you saw Stephen King

00:03:52.497 --> 00:03:53.866
tweeting about your book?

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:54.967
-Not bad.

00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:57.937
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.937 --> 00:04:00.840
I grew up a total
Stephen King fanatic,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.810
so basically from ages 11 to 14,
I read everything

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.311
the guy had published to
that point and continued to.

00:04:06.311 --> 00:04:09.348
But, you know, as a 12-year-old
up until 3:00 a.m.,

00:04:09.348 --> 00:04:11.517
reading "Gerald's Game,"
unfortunately,

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:14.854
I think that informed
my personality too much.

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:18.090
But, look, it's such an honor,
and I wrote a letter to him.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.492
He tweeted about "Ohio."

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:21.460
I wrote a letter to him, like,
sent it to my editor

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.996
to send to his editor,
just basically saying like,

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:26.099
"Look, dude,
you are one of the reasons

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:27.634
I wanted to become a writer.

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:30.236
Like, whatever you could do,
that mesmerized me as a kid."

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:31.437
-That's really cool.

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:33.405
I want to --
mostly just because I feel like

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:34.506
this one is gonna really
stay with me,

00:04:34.506 --> 00:04:35.875
a second tweet about it.

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:39.812
"More terrifying than
'The Stand' because it's real."

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:42.948
So that really
made my bones hurt.

00:04:42.948 --> 00:04:46.052
-I just want to -- When I die,
I want to be remembered as

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:47.052
the guy who freaked out
Stephen King.

00:04:47.052 --> 00:04:49.889
-Yeah, exactly.

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:52.358
Yeah, that's a good thing
to shoot for.

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:53.659
I do want to go back to
something he said

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:55.894
in the first tweet,
which is he ended by saying,

00:04:55.894 --> 00:04:58.631
you know, again, terrifying,
but he also said uplifting.

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:00.333
So, you have spent
all this time.

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:01.634
You've talked to scientists.

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.902
You wrote this book about how
bad things could be.

00:05:03.902 --> 00:05:06.505
And, yet, I think it's important
to say you still have hope.

00:05:06.505 --> 00:05:08.474
Where does your hope come from?

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:11.510
-Yeah, okay, so,
it's not, like, in the course of

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.446
writing this novel,
I didn't feel

00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:15.781
overwhelming constant despair.

00:05:15.781 --> 00:05:17.449
I won't lie about that.

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:21.253
I think where I arrived,
though, is that, oddly enough,

00:05:21.253 --> 00:05:23.689
there is joy and there
is privilege in being alive

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:26.259
in a time when
our actions as citizens

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:29.128
and as agents of history will
echo for what is effectively

00:05:29.128 --> 00:05:31.230
the eternity of
human civilization.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.732
You know, we have these
precious few years left

00:05:33.732 --> 00:05:36.168
to set in motion epic change,

00:05:36.168 --> 00:05:38.671
and we have every tool
we need to rapidly decarbonize

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:39.839
the global economy.

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:42.007
Renewables are
the cheapest energy, right?

00:05:42.007 --> 00:05:44.710
We have the I.R.A. now,
the Inflation Reduction Act,

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.779
and this puts power into
the hands of every --

00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:50.249
everybody, state, local,
like, school boards.

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:51.583
Like, look down at
your school board.

00:05:51.583 --> 00:05:54.687
Like, down to that level,
we have power to decarbonize.

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.788
We crush demand for fossil fuels

00:05:56.788 --> 00:05:58.724
by rapidly
electrifying everything.

00:05:58.724 --> 00:06:01.427
We keep pressure on the
big banks to stop financing

00:06:01.427 --> 00:06:02.795
the destruction of our world,

00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:05.397
and we vote every election
no matter what

00:06:05.397 --> 00:06:07.566
and we vote for people
who actually treat this crisis

00:06:07.566 --> 00:06:09.435
like it's the
biggest emergency we have.

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:10.603
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:13.606
-That's something I think
we can all agree on.

00:06:13.606 --> 00:06:18.243
It really is -- I can't speak to
how, back-to-back times,

00:06:18.243 --> 00:06:20.646
you've just written these
really long, wonderful books

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:21.681
that are really hard
to put down.

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:22.814
It's such an accomplishment.

00:06:22.814 --> 00:06:23.949
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:25.718
-Thank you.
-It's so great to meet you.

00:06:25.718 --> 00:06:27.153
Stephen Markley, everybody.

00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:29.822
"The Deluge" is available now
wherever you buy your books,

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:31.623
but please support local
and independent bookstores.

00:06:31.623 --> 00:06:32.725
We'll be right back.

